WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.920
Hello everyone, welcome back to another episode of the Grand Prixquel Podcast where we discuss

00:03.920 --> 00:05.680
all things feeder series.

00:05.680 --> 00:11.160
On today's episode, we're going to be recapping the F2 and F3 weekends at Barcelona.

00:11.160 --> 00:19.760
Your hosts for today are myself, Robin, Peter and Evan.

00:19.760 --> 00:26.760
So of course before the F2 and F3 weekends actually started, we had some rookie FP1s.

00:26.760 --> 00:32.120
So we had six teams taking F2 series drivers into their teams, so there was Dino Beganovic

00:32.120 --> 00:41.560
for Ferrari, Leonardo Fornaroli for McLaren, Luke Browning for Williams, Colton Herta

00:41.560 --> 00:47.600
for Cadillac, Paul Aron for Audi, Ayumu Iwasa for Red Bull and Fred Vesti for Mercedes.

00:47.600 --> 00:53.880
So I mean, some of these drivers showed quite a good pace, I mean in particular I'd say

00:54.080 --> 01:00.440
Fornaroli and Aron, Beganovic, they were quite, they finished practice in the top 10.

01:00.440 --> 01:05.200
And especially Paul Aron as he finished ahead of Bortoleto in practice.

01:05.200 --> 01:11.040
I think it's interesting that Ferrari put Dino in the car and not Camara.

01:11.040 --> 01:16.560
Maybe they'll give Camara FP1 sessions later on in the year, I just think that's interesting.

01:16.560 --> 01:22.360
You know, me too, maybe he'll do some with Haas, because if you think about last year,

01:22.600 --> 01:25.400
Bearman did them with Haas as well, so.

01:25.400 --> 01:28.400
Who did the Ferrari ones last year? I don't remember.

01:28.400 --> 01:32.600
But the interesting thing is that with Bearman, it was clear that he's going to do this many

01:32.600 --> 01:38.280
FP's, he's very clearly our driver, he's a big announcement when he first did it in 2024.

01:38.280 --> 01:45.800
With Camara, he tested an F1 car, but I mean, maybe in Brazil, because there's no sprint

01:45.800 --> 01:51.880
race in Brazil this year, so surely in Brazil they'd give it to him in front of his home fans,

01:52.680 --> 01:55.160
Maybe Ferrari just thinks he's that bad.

01:55.160 --> 01:57.480
Maybe that's just why he didn't get the session.

01:58.200 --> 02:02.200
Yeah, maybe what happened in Monaco. It’s just right in their minds.

02:02.760 --> 02:06.040
But it really makes the question of how good is he?

02:06.040 --> 02:09.160
And then Fornaroli, I mean, also, 67.

02:09.880 --> 02:10.680
67.

02:12.360 --> 02:14.040
He drove the 67 car.

02:15.400 --> 02:19.000
I don't know why they, McLaren changed the number, but they did.

02:19.000 --> 02:22.200
Is there no one on the team who told them that 67 is a meme?

02:22.200 --> 02:24.520
Or maybe they did it on purpose.

02:24.520 --> 02:26.840
Zak Brown needs to appeal to the kids.

02:26.840 --> 02:29.000
He has to expand the fanbase.

02:29.000 --> 02:30.200
67.

02:30.200 --> 02:32.040
And then of course, Luke Browning.

02:33.720 --> 02:37.480
Well, he would have been in the car, but he wasn't in the car, because there's an electrical

02:37.480 --> 02:38.200
issue.

02:38.200 --> 02:41.720
I think if I remember right, there was a connector in the car that wasn't right.

02:41.720 --> 02:46.120
The car was entirely torn apart and they tried to find it, and they never ended up finding it.

02:46.680 --> 02:51.480
And I just think it's really unfortunate that Luke just didn't get a run after so much

02:51.480 --> 02:57.400
press and hype through other, I guess, media organizations.

02:57.400 --> 03:01.320
I think PlanetF1 might have done an interview with him and other media organizations as well.

03:02.280 --> 03:05.160
I think there was a lot of hype for Luke to be doing this FP1, and then, well,

03:05.800 --> 03:07.480
car electrical issues said no.

03:08.760 --> 03:10.280
And that was that, I guess.

03:10.280 --> 03:13.080
But at least that means he gets to do another session elsewhere.

03:13.080 --> 03:19.400
But there's not really too many amazingly great locations he could do, like more sessions

03:19.400 --> 03:19.640
at.

03:19.640 --> 03:24.040
Because it's been confirmed he's doing it next week, no, well, the week after at Red Bull

03:24.040 --> 03:24.840
Ring.

03:24.840 --> 03:28.600
Yeah, that one was announced with his Barcelona announcement as well.

03:28.600 --> 03:29.800
So we know that for sure.

03:30.760 --> 03:36.680
But I guess the replacement to whatever Barcelona was meant to be, the obvious one,

03:36.680 --> 03:39.800
well, if there wasn’t a sprint weekend for whatever reason, the obvious one would have

03:39.800 --> 03:41.960
been the British Grand Prix, because he's British.

03:42.200 --> 03:43.880
You know, home crowd, whatever.

03:43.880 --> 03:47.240
But apparently it's a sprint weekend this year.

03:47.960 --> 03:51.640
So I'm just trying to see where else he would fit in another one, because he needs to do

03:51.640 --> 03:53.000
it in, I think, unless...

03:54.040 --> 03:56.040
I think this one already counted for him.

03:57.000 --> 03:58.120
No, I don't think it did.

03:58.840 --> 04:01.720
There's no way, though, because he had no running at all.

04:03.160 --> 04:04.200
He was just entered into it.

04:04.200 --> 04:05.880
He never ran at all.

04:05.880 --> 04:11.080
So from what I heard or am hearing, just in general, people were saying that it hasn't

04:11.080 --> 04:11.960
counted.

04:11.960 --> 04:13.480
But I guess we have to see.

04:13.480 --> 04:14.520
I heard the opposite.

04:14.520 --> 04:16.520
I heard people say that it did count.

04:17.480 --> 04:22.600
It would be pretty idiotic, though, because the point of the rookie FP1 session is to

04:22.600 --> 04:26.520
give the rookie driver the chance to drive, is from what I'm understanding is what it's

04:26.520 --> 04:27.000
meant to be.

04:27.640 --> 04:32.040
And so really, all that's happened on the Barcelona weekend for Luke and Williams is

04:32.040 --> 04:34.200
that Alex Albon just didn't get to drive FP1.

04:34.760 --> 04:40.200
It's not a case of, like, actually there's any actual rookie driving testing in that

04:40.200 --> 04:40.760
at all.

04:41.720 --> 04:48.360
Maybe Luke sabotaged the car on purpose because he couldn't run FP1.

04:48.360 --> 04:50.920
So he just, like, unscrewed Alex's camera.

04:50.920 --> 04:54.600
And that's why whatever happened in the F1 race happened.

04:55.160 --> 04:56.920
My boy would never do that.

04:56.920 --> 04:58.120
Your boy wants a Williams seat.

04:58.120 --> 04:58.920
My boy would never do that.

04:58.920 --> 05:03.080
Don't you dare ever accuse him of that ever again.

05:04.040 --> 05:07.160
I will curse Alex Powell personally, even more than he's already cursed.

05:08.120 --> 05:10.200
What brought Alex Powell into this?

05:11.160 --> 05:11.720
Too bad.

05:11.720 --> 05:12.760
You brought Luke into this.

05:12.760 --> 05:15.880
You just said that Luke sabotaged the car and he definitely did not.

05:15.880 --> 05:17.240
I'm bringing Alex Powell into this.

05:18.280 --> 05:21.400
So next up, we had Colton Herta for Cadillac.

05:21.400 --> 05:26.600
Obviously, he's just getting the super license points because for every 100 kilometers he

05:26.600 --> 05:27.720
runs, he gets one point.

05:28.920 --> 05:32.200
And he's currently at 34 points, so he needs six more points.

05:33.160 --> 05:37.640
Hopefully he can aim for top 10 finish in F2 to get a few more points,

05:37.640 --> 05:39.720
but that is the aim for him.

05:40.600 --> 05:46.600
I think if he does four FP1s, he needs a P8 in F2.

05:46.600 --> 05:48.040
A P9 should be fine.

05:48.760 --> 05:50.440
Oh yeah, P9, sorry.

05:52.360 --> 05:55.240
So I don't know how possible a P9 is.

05:57.080 --> 05:58.600
He's P12 at the moment.

05:59.560 --> 06:01.400
He should have been higher than that.

06:01.880 --> 06:05.080
Paul Aron was quite interesting for Audi.

06:05.080 --> 06:10.680
I think they eventually will have Freddie in, but Freddie is only 17 at the moment.

06:10.680 --> 06:15.240
He does have the super license points because he's won a lot of racing series.

06:16.360 --> 06:23.800
Can't they apply for an exemption like Mercedes did for Kimi in 2024?

06:23.800 --> 06:28.360
There's no point there if he's not going to be an F1 till 2028 anyways.

06:28.360 --> 06:30.840
Yeah, but he might as well get the experience.

06:30.840 --> 06:32.520
And he turns 18 in August.

06:33.400 --> 06:37.800
I'm curious to know why Alpine lends Aron to Audi.

06:37.800 --> 06:44.360
It's just interesting why would they not run Aron at Alpine because that's where he's signed to.

06:45.000 --> 06:49.800
I mean, Aron did some stuff for when they were Sauber last year as well, didn't he?

06:50.360 --> 06:52.520
I mean, yeah, maybe there's an agreement there already.

06:52.520 --> 06:53.640
It's just interesting to me.

06:55.880 --> 06:57.320
Audi had to get somebody.

06:57.320 --> 06:58.360
They need at least one person.

06:58.360 --> 07:03.960
They have no drivers in their academy right now that can do FP1 sessions on.

07:03.960 --> 07:05.640
So I guess they had to borrow somebody.

07:05.640 --> 07:09.480
It's just interesting that Alpine decided to move theirs till later, I suppose.

07:09.480 --> 07:12.520
Because I mean, almost every team did something here.

07:12.520 --> 07:14.120
So like, you know.

07:14.120 --> 07:16.040
It's just Briatore being Briatore.

07:16.920 --> 07:22.040
Audi or, well, at the time, Sauber ran Robert Shwartzman at one point.

07:22.040 --> 07:24.760
But he was never going to be with Ferrari.

07:25.320 --> 07:26.040
They did, right?

07:26.040 --> 07:26.840
I forgot about that.

07:27.400 --> 07:32.440
That's back when they had Ferrari engines, though, here, it kind of made sense.

07:32.440 --> 07:35.320
I'm going Wikipedia to confirm that Robert Shwartzman actually did do it,

07:35.320 --> 07:36.520
because I feel like he did.

07:36.520 --> 07:37.240
He did.

07:37.240 --> 07:38.280
I remember.

07:38.280 --> 07:39.880
And he got a penalty for it.

07:39.880 --> 07:45.640
He got a penalty during that session, a grid penalty that he would have to serve in his

07:46.440 --> 07:48.840
next race, which is never.

07:49.640 --> 07:53.000
I can love what you, it's so funny to me when you go on Wikipedia,

07:53.000 --> 07:54.840
because I can tell when he fell off.

07:54.840 --> 07:57.000
Because in 2023, he was testing Ferrari.

07:57.000 --> 07:59.400
But in 2024, he's straight down to Sauber.

07:59.400 --> 08:01.480
Like, he should have been dumped at Sauber to do testing for them.

08:02.280 --> 08:03.640
I can see what the downfall was for him.

08:04.520 --> 08:05.240
That's lovely.

08:05.960 --> 08:07.560
So moving on.

08:08.200 --> 08:14.200
We had Ayumu Iwasa for Red Bull, which I would have thought they'd bring in Tsolov.

08:14.200 --> 08:17.800
But maybe Tsolov is going to be there for VCARB at some point.

08:17.800 --> 08:20.920
I think also it might just be to do with they wanted to get data,

08:20.920 --> 08:25.720
and Iwasa is arguably a more developed driver than Tsolov is.

08:26.520 --> 08:28.360
So I think that could be part of it as well.

08:29.400 --> 08:32.360
It's like how we've had Fred Vesti for Mercedes for those two.

08:32.360 --> 08:33.960
It clearly is just data.

08:34.760 --> 08:39.960
Yeah, I mean, when is Fred not doing the FP1s for Mercedes.

08:39.960 --> 08:42.200
At least he’s no longer locked in the simulator.

08:42.920 --> 08:44.120
Let him see the daylight.

08:44.680 --> 08:48.440
The Mercedes is also locked into having to use Fred anyway to do their

08:49.400 --> 08:54.360
rookie testing, or rookie FP1 testing, because their academy team has no drivers that could

08:54.360 --> 08:55.400
possibly do it.

08:55.400 --> 08:59.960
Yeah, every single one of their academy drivers is Formula Regional or below.

09:00.600 --> 09:02.760
Imagine if they got Rashid Al Dhaheri in the car.

09:04.600 --> 09:06.520
Abu Dhabi, you know, Emirati driver.

09:06.520 --> 09:08.680
Does he have the super license points for that?

09:09.240 --> 09:11.320
If he wins FREC, he will.

09:12.040 --> 09:13.240
If he wins FREC.

09:13.880 --> 09:16.680
I mean, he's leading the championship, isn't he?

09:17.320 --> 09:19.000
You heard this here first.

09:19.000 --> 09:21.320
Rashid Al Dhaheri doing free practice sessions.

09:23.960 --> 09:28.280
Okay, so do we want to move on to the actual F2 weekend?

09:29.320 --> 09:31.160
Yes, we'd love to do that.

09:31.160 --> 09:35.160
So in practice, F2 up first.

09:35.160 --> 09:39.000
We had Noel Leon going off in practice causing the red flag.

09:39.800 --> 09:42.680
One of many Campos’ to have incidents this weekend.

09:43.640 --> 09:47.240
And I think that the Campos loved that turn three gravel.

09:47.880 --> 09:53.560
Quali was normal-ish, except for the fact that Villagomez was P5.

09:53.560 --> 10:00.360
Yeah, and Camara had quite a big margin on the field, but that does need to be

10:00.360 --> 10:04.840
caveated with that Miyata crashed at the end bringing out the red flag, of course.

10:05.400 --> 10:08.680
Yeah, there were a bunch of drivers that were improving at the time.

10:08.840 --> 10:14.440
One of them being Laurens Van Hoepen, improving to be like, I think based on the

10:14.440 --> 10:16.040
sector time, sector 2 split.

10:17.160 --> 10:19.400
He was only like a tenth or so off Camara.

10:20.760 --> 10:26.760
Duerksen got a five-place penalty, I think it was five places, for not respecting yellow flags.

10:27.640 --> 10:30.440
And his fans were obviously not very happy.

10:31.080 --> 10:36.680
Duerksen keeps getting penalized and it's entertaining as someone who doesn't like Invicta.

10:36.680 --> 10:40.760
I mean, the thing is, they need to balance out their favoritism towards Camara with,

10:41.480 --> 10:44.760
you know, maybe not being so nice to Duerksen, that’s all it is.

10:44.760 --> 10:48.760
You can't be nice to both Invicta cars, you need to make up for it somehow.

10:48.760 --> 10:52.040
We had Herta qualify in the top ten, which was very nice.

10:52.840 --> 10:55.560
Um, not very nice what happened later, but-

10:55.560 --> 10:58.040
We had really big expectations for him.

10:58.680 --> 11:03.000
I mean, the sprint race, I mean, Kush Maini, what the hell?

11:03.000 --> 11:04.680
What is in that ART?

11:04.680 --> 11:07.720
Seven-second win by Kush Maini of all people.

11:08.360 --> 11:10.920
That ART is powered by money and money only.

11:10.920 --> 11:14.520
Cause there is no way that, like, from one year to another year,

11:14.520 --> 11:15.800
that they've improved that much.

11:15.800 --> 11:17.960
It is clearly just money, I fear.

11:17.960 --> 11:19.960
Oh, and look at their drivers as well, like,

11:20.680 --> 11:25.160
I mean, you've gone from having Victor Martins to Kush Maini.

11:26.040 --> 11:27.960
It's really a downgrade.

11:28.840 --> 11:30.600
It's the Victor Martins luck.

11:30.600 --> 11:33.240
As soon as he left, ART locked in.

11:34.200 --> 11:35.880
And now he's going to Alpine Endurance,

11:35.880 --> 11:38.520
and Alpine Endurance aren't going to be there next year, I mean.

11:39.320 --> 11:41.000
It's the Victor Martins luck.

11:41.560 --> 11:45.560
Also Maini, he got fastest lap, and his fastest lap was like

11:45.560 --> 11:49.080
three tenths faster than the next closest fastest lap by another driver.

11:49.800 --> 11:50.600
Oh my god.

11:51.240 --> 11:53.560
And yeah, we’ll leave it to Colton Herta.

11:55.000 --> 11:57.960
I mean, it was going really well for him until the final lap,

11:58.520 --> 12:00.440
cause he held onto his tyres,

12:00.520 --> 12:02.200
he made a really good overtake on Tsolov,

12:02.200 --> 12:04.440
with this very kind of Indycar-style overtake.

12:05.240 --> 12:08.600
You know, I kind of thought, is this finally the moment?

12:08.600 --> 12:09.800
And then he bottled it!

12:10.440 --> 12:12.760
In the most Colton Herta way ever.

12:12.760 --> 12:14.280
Threw away a podium.

12:14.280 --> 12:15.720
I am so mad.

12:16.840 --> 12:20.680
Colton, go back to Indycar, the Indycars call for you.

12:21.400 --> 12:23.400
I mean, he still bottled it there.

12:23.400 --> 12:26.520
Yeah, but it's less painful than seeing him,

12:27.480 --> 12:29.160
it's less painful than seeing him,

12:30.600 --> 12:33.400
throw away a podium with Kush Maini on it.

12:34.120 --> 12:36.920
I don't think too much else happened in the sprint,

12:36.920 --> 12:39.080
apart from the Duerksen-Stenshorne incident,

12:39.080 --> 12:41.880
where it was like more Duerksen going off

12:41.880 --> 12:44.360
and Stenshorne getting penalized for it, I can't lie.

12:45.000 --> 12:47.640
Well, Stenshorne got a penalty for it,

12:47.640 --> 12:50.040
10 seconds, not 5 seconds like it says in the doc.

12:50.040 --> 12:51.560
Oh, 10, my bad.

12:52.360 --> 12:54.440
But yeah, I don't actually remember the incident,

12:55.160 --> 12:58.280
so I couldn't tell you if it was, if the penalty was deserved or not.

12:58.280 --> 13:02.040
I mean, they were kind of fighting for position,

13:02.040 --> 13:04.440
but then, you know, Stenshorne’s ahead at the apex,

13:05.000 --> 13:06.840
Duerksen tries to go in and he kind of,

13:06.840 --> 13:08.520
they make a bit of contact I think then.

13:09.240 --> 13:10.840
Duerksen must really hate the Rodins.

13:11.800 --> 13:15.640
I mean, or maybe Stenshorne is just learning from his teammate Alex Dunne,

13:15.640 --> 13:19.640
although Dunne has finally had two clean weekends in a row,

13:19.640 --> 13:21.240
I think we need to applaud him for it.

13:21.880 --> 13:24.920
What a concept of a racing driver being able to keep it clean,

13:26.120 --> 13:27.560
especially Alex Dunne.

13:29.080 --> 13:32.600
Out of every single driver, you least expect Alex Dunne keeping it clean,

13:33.720 --> 13:37.800
that deserves, he deserves the championship just for that, quite frankly.

13:37.800 --> 13:38.440
Exactly.

13:39.320 --> 13:41.240
I mean, but he's proven though,

13:41.240 --> 13:43.720
when he keeps it clean, he does get the results.

13:43.720 --> 13:46.760
I mean, moving on to the feature race, you know, P3.

13:47.400 --> 13:51.000
That fight between him and Tsolov was so good,

13:51.000 --> 13:55.640
and I was so surprised that it didn't end in a collision or any contact at all,

13:55.640 --> 13:59.880
they were just both of them racing really cleanly, and it was so good.

14:00.440 --> 14:04.440
He's actually P2 in the feature race, not P3 because of the-

14:04.440 --> 14:07.080
Yeah, Tsolov got penalized post race.

14:07.960 --> 14:11.160
I think he ended up behind Mini after the penalty,

14:11.880 --> 14:15.240
so the podium ended up being Camara Dunne Mini.

14:15.240 --> 14:18.840
I mean, from the start, I mean, Dunne got the jump on the start,

14:19.560 --> 14:23.960
Camara, I mean, that Invicta, can we please investigate it?

14:23.960 --> 14:27.720
We need to launch a formal investigation on that Invicta because what the hell?

14:28.360 --> 14:31.000
How do you go 22 laps on softs?

14:31.000 --> 14:35.160
The commentators were saying that the maximum last year was 16 laps.

14:35.160 --> 14:36.440
How do you do 22?

14:37.240 --> 14:38.360
And still be fast?

14:38.920 --> 14:40.680
It's not like he was massively, you know,

14:41.560 --> 14:44.840
you know, like, conserving his pace, saving his tires.

14:45.640 --> 14:46.840
FIA investigate.

14:47.400 --> 14:48.200
Please, please.

14:50.120 --> 14:51.720
There's something shady going on.

14:52.440 --> 14:57.720
Dunn and Mini both got, like, hit by traffic after they pit,

14:57.720 --> 15:00.440
which really did help Camara, but-

15:01.480 --> 15:03.720
But he was still doing semi-decent lap times.

15:04.600 --> 15:05.560
It is so weird.

15:06.200 --> 15:10.920
And then we had, yeah, Tsolov, I mean, Tsolov did the alternate strategy.

15:10.920 --> 15:15.640
Most drivers, they went soft hard, Tsolov went hard soft.

15:16.360 --> 15:19.080
Yeah, so, I mean, he was really making up time at the end,

15:19.080 --> 15:24.040
and he would have been P2 if he didn't overtake Mini off track.

15:24.840 --> 15:28.440
Did you see, like, I don't remember whether it was in the Steward's Doc

15:28.440 --> 15:31.640
or whether it was in the article on the F2 page,

15:31.640 --> 15:36.600
but they were talking about how they needed to use high resolution cameras

15:36.600 --> 15:39.560
to see whether Tsolov went off track or not.

15:40.280 --> 15:42.600
Does that imply that the cameras aren't high resolution then?

15:43.720 --> 15:44.360
Like, what is that?

15:44.360 --> 15:46.200
Does that imply that the cameras are not high resolution?

15:46.920 --> 15:48.680
Oh, you need to use high resolution cameras.

15:49.400 --> 15:53.800
Surely they can solve this issue by just having an automated system which can detect.

15:54.440 --> 15:59.400
The issue is that, like, the only, just like, the issue of that is that

15:59.400 --> 16:01.160
it's dependent on GPS, potentially.

16:01.160 --> 16:04.520
The only most accurate way we have to determine, like, location,

16:04.520 --> 16:06.200
like, the locational position is GPS.

16:06.840 --> 16:09.880
And GPS is also horrifically unreliable.

16:09.880 --> 16:13.400
It is, like, the most reliable, the least reliable technology we have,

16:13.400 --> 16:14.760
or, like, something like that.

16:14.760 --> 16:17.640
And the only other alternative, to me at least,

16:17.640 --> 16:20.280
is that you have the lines on the track,

16:20.280 --> 16:22.680
like, a camera on the nose cone or something,

16:22.680 --> 16:26.040
that can, like, do some, like, magic AI, whatever,

16:26.040 --> 16:28.200
to determine if a wheel is, like, oh...

16:29.640 --> 16:32.520
The point is that you can't really do that without, like,

16:32.520 --> 16:33.960
there's actually no way to do it.

16:33.960 --> 16:35.720
They have to manually actually look at the footage,

16:35.720 --> 16:38.920
which is why I find it funny they said high resolution cameras,

16:38.920 --> 16:41.000
because that implies that cameras aren't high resolution at all.

16:41.720 --> 16:47.640
Apparently, Tsolov was only very marginally off the line,

16:48.920 --> 16:53.560
so they had to use, like, high resolution cameras, apparently.

16:54.360 --> 16:58.520
My question is, what qualifies as a high resolution camera?

16:58.520 --> 17:01.560
Like, did they have, like, were one of the TV directing cameras,

17:01.560 --> 17:07.160
like, one of the TV cameras pointed, like, pretty, like, right behind Tsolov?

17:07.800 --> 17:11.560
Like, what do they mean, we use high definition cameras,

17:11.560 --> 17:12.920
or high resolution cameras?

17:12.920 --> 17:14.600
What do you normally use, then?

17:15.240 --> 17:19.000
Yeah, to my knowledge, they determine, like,

17:19.000 --> 17:21.400
a lot of the decisions are determined based off, like,

17:21.400 --> 17:24.440
the cameras, like, on the track, like, the track cameras,

17:24.440 --> 17:25.800
as well as maybe the T-cam.

17:25.800 --> 17:29.240
Does this have somebody recording on, like, their iPhone 6?

17:29.240 --> 17:31.720
I'm genuinely curious, like, what do you mean?

17:31.720 --> 17:37.000
Like, why did they have to make a distinction between

17:37.160 --> 17:39.960
like, the fact that it was a high resolution camera specifically?

17:39.960 --> 17:42.040
That's also semantics that doesn't really matter,

17:42.040 --> 17:44.040
but yeah, Formula 2, please explain.

17:44.040 --> 17:45.000
I'd love to know.

17:45.000 --> 17:47.080
Average F2 experience.

17:47.080 --> 17:49.240
I think other than that, of course, Ollie Goethe.

17:49.240 --> 17:51.080
I mean, he's had a bit of a painful race.

17:51.720 --> 17:54.520
He didn't even do the formation lap.

17:54.520 --> 17:59.400
As he gets pushed, and then he makes it about 100 meters outside the pit exit,

17:59.400 --> 18:00.760
and the car dies again.

18:01.880 --> 18:05.480
He's just had been having pretty bad luck in general, honestly, just like...

18:05.480 --> 18:06.840
I saw a post.

18:06.840 --> 18:07.960
On Twitter.

18:07.960 --> 18:12.280
That was like, he somehow got McLaren's reliability,

18:12.280 --> 18:15.400
because the car is orange.

18:15.400 --> 18:19.080
I'm not calling it papaya, because the MP car is not papaya,

18:19.080 --> 18:23.160
but it's almost McLaren papaya.

18:24.200 --> 18:28.520
So somehow, the McLaren reliability has transferred over to him.

18:29.160 --> 18:30.440
I think Herta.

18:30.440 --> 18:32.280
I mean, the pain continued in this way.

18:33.240 --> 18:34.200
Yeah, I...

18:34.200 --> 18:35.320
What even happened to him?

18:35.320 --> 18:37.880
He was running in the top 10, and then suddenly he wasn't.

18:38.520 --> 18:40.360
The pit cycle messed him up.

18:40.920 --> 18:43.080
He basically got undercut by, like...

18:43.800 --> 18:47.240
He got undercut by Stenshorne and Beganovic,

18:48.200 --> 18:50.920
and then he just couldn't make up the positions.

18:50.920 --> 18:52.600
We need to nuke HiTech.

18:53.160 --> 18:54.760
It was going really well, I remember.

18:54.760 --> 18:56.520
He overtakes Tso-

18:56.520 --> 18:58.360
He's looking that he may overtake Varrone,

18:58.360 --> 19:00.760
but then the whole pit cycle just messed him up.

19:02.040 --> 19:03.960
And then he had all the people on the,

19:03.960 --> 19:06.040
who went on the alternative strategy.

19:06.920 --> 19:08.200
He went hard-soft,

19:08.200 --> 19:09.160
and the end was coming,

19:09.160 --> 19:11.880
and the naturally softs are a lot faster than the hards.

19:13.240 --> 19:15.400
So they were just coming through, and yeah, it was just...

19:16.040 --> 19:18.200
I mean, at least the sprint race showed something.

19:19.400 --> 19:23.560
I hope that if he can qualify on the top 10 again next week,

19:24.840 --> 19:26.440
at Redbull Ring, and of course,

19:27.320 --> 19:29.480
that is a great circuit for overtaking.

19:30.120 --> 19:32.200
It's also a great circuit for track limits.

19:33.720 --> 19:34.600
Colton…

19:34.600 --> 19:35.480
Oh, and gravel.

19:37.000 --> 19:38.360
Speaking of track limits,

19:39.000 --> 19:41.880
we had a couple penalties over the weekend for track limits.

19:42.600 --> 19:47.240
Shields got two penalties during the feature.

19:47.960 --> 19:52.200
Bilinski and Villagomez both got two during the sprint.

19:52.840 --> 19:55.080
If we had bad track limits this weekend,

19:55.080 --> 19:56.840
imagine how it's going to be at Austria.

19:57.560 --> 20:00.920
But are they going to be using the high-resolution cameras in Austria?

20:02.280 --> 20:05.240
They're going to be using an iPhone 5 from 2012

20:05.240 --> 20:07.080
to do all the TV direction,

20:07.080 --> 20:09.480
so that's why they need the high-resolution cameras.

20:09.480 --> 20:12.440
They have a camera positioned at each exit, just to see.

20:13.560 --> 20:16.920
There's probably some guy out in the stands recording it,

20:16.920 --> 20:17.960
and that's what they're using.

20:19.000 --> 20:21.880
I'm still stuck on the high-resolution camera thing, by the way,

20:21.880 --> 20:25.160
because there are like four different types of cameras

20:25.160 --> 20:26.600
that are used around a circuit.

20:26.600 --> 20:29.160
And yeah, the high-resolution camera

20:29.960 --> 20:31.720
is a completely different camera to those.

20:32.680 --> 20:36.200
Like, so your TV camera, you got the ground cameras,

20:36.200 --> 20:38.680
the ones in the ground, you got the T-cam,

20:38.680 --> 20:40.680
and then you have maybe the drone or whatever,

20:41.560 --> 20:45.400
or the flying, whatever, the suspended camera.

20:48.200 --> 20:50.520
Where does the high-resolution camera fit in, then?

20:50.520 --> 20:52.680
But whatever, I think I've gone on too much

20:52.680 --> 20:54.280
about the high-resolution camera thing.

20:54.280 --> 20:55.640
I guess we'll find out next weekend.

20:56.280 --> 20:59.240
So in terms of the championship,

21:00.600 --> 21:04.520
before Tsolov’s penalty, he was leading the championship,

21:04.520 --> 21:06.760
but then he got penalized, so now he's not.

21:07.400 --> 21:11.080
So Mini still leads the championship on 86 points,

21:11.080 --> 21:18.040
Tsolov in P2 on 80, Camara on 69, and Dunne on 67.

21:18.680 --> 21:23.640
I think it's really funny that Camara’s on 69,

21:23.640 --> 21:25.720
and Dunne is on 67.

21:27.400 --> 21:35.480
Then Noel Leon is in P5 on 54 points, and Beganovic P6 on 53.

21:36.200 --> 21:40.200
All I’m going to say is our curse continues.

21:40.200 --> 21:44.920
We said last week that Tsolov would surely overtake Mini

21:44.920 --> 21:46.920
for the championship lead in Barcelona.

21:46.920 --> 21:48.920
Look, technically he did.

21:49.800 --> 21:50.680
He did at one point.

21:50.680 --> 21:53.880
He did for a couple hours, for like an hour.

21:54.600 --> 21:57.560
So was the curse really working there?

21:58.680 --> 21:59.240
I don't know.

21:59.240 --> 22:02.600
I'm only saying this because I'm the one who said that Tsolov

22:03.400 --> 22:05.080
should be ahead of Mini.

22:05.080 --> 22:07.080
The curse really did work,

22:07.080 --> 22:10.440
because he had the happiness of leading the championship for an hour,

22:10.440 --> 22:11.880
and then it got taken away from him.

22:11.880 --> 22:14.600
But I think the championship

22:14.600 --> 22:16.520
fight is going to be between the top four.

22:17.160 --> 22:21.560
I think Beganovic could work his way in, because I remember the start of the season,

22:21.560 --> 22:26.120
he was quite fast, he was just unlucky, and maybe he can work himself in somehow.

22:26.120 --> 22:27.800
I think.

22:28.440 --> 22:36.840
For once, the top four drivers in the standings are the top four best drivers in the championship.

22:36.840 --> 22:40.200
I think it's evened out with any bad luck and stuff.

22:43.240 --> 22:48.360
Even though it could seem like a big gap of 20 points, one DNF turns it around completely.

22:50.280 --> 22:54.520
Given F2 and Mecachrome, you never know who's going to explode next.

22:54.520 --> 22:56.440
That sounds so ominous.

22:57.800 --> 22:59.640
You never know who's going to explode next.

22:59.640 --> 23:02.120
Am I going to get blamed for whoever gets

23:02.120 --> 23:03.720
Mecachromed next week?

23:03.720 --> 23:11.240
Team standings, Campos leads on 134 points,

23:12.200 --> 23:20.600
Rodin is in P2 on 115, MP is in P3 on 98, then Invicta on 90, and Dams on 65.

23:20.600 --> 23:23.240
Again no surprises there,

23:23.320 --> 23:28.840
cause I mean, like Rodin, they probably have the strongest driver lineup with Dunne and Stenshorne

23:28.840 --> 23:33.640
overall I'd say, and even Campos, I mean, Tsolov is good, Leon is solid enough.

23:34.600 --> 23:40.360
So I'd say things have now quite evened out in F2, cause this is what, our fifth weekend I think?

23:40.360 --> 23:42.840
I think so.

23:42.840 --> 23:45.640
Yeah, you’ve now had enough representative

23:45.640 --> 23:52.360
weekends for it to eventually even out, cause it's not like after Miami when you only had two

23:52.360 --> 23:57.640
weekends there's not enough has happened to fairly judge, but now there are clear trends,

23:57.640 --> 24:03.720
and I do still feel like Tsolov is the fastest, and probably the best driver in the championship.

24:03.720 --> 24:07.240
Dunne is the fastest, he's just not consistent,

24:08.040 --> 24:11.560
and by consistent I mean he keeps crashing, but he's stopped that.

24:11.560 --> 24:14.920
But if he keeps it tidy, if he keeps it up,

24:15.720 --> 24:21.480
he could have a solid championship result, and if he does really well,

24:21.480 --> 24:30.120
I mean, and if he does really well, he could put pressure on Alpine and Franco Colapinto cause

24:30.120 --> 24:35.080
he has motivation to do it as well, but then again that could make him lose his head,

24:35.080 --> 24:36.520
the thought of an F1 seat.

24:36.520 --> 24:42.520
We’ll have to see, who knows, maybe when F1 Silly Season

24:42.520 --> 24:47.560
properly gets started, we'll see whether that gets to his head or not.

24:48.440 --> 24:50.840
And then next up, we had of course the

24:50.840 --> 24:58.440
Formula 3 weekend. We knew after Monaco, in classic Grand Prixquel luck, the day after we

24:58.440 --> 25:02.920
record, hours after we record, the Benavide replacement gets announced.

25:02.920 --> 25:05.880
We were literally talking on the episode about how

25:06.760 --> 25:12.040
it would be really annoying if they announced it straight after we recorded, and then not even

25:12.040 --> 25:17.720
hours after we record. It wasn't really announced, we just saw the entry list,

25:17.720 --> 25:21.720
which was really annoying, and I said on the episode that I was going to cry if it happened,

25:21.720 --> 25:22.840
so I will cry.

25:22.840 --> 25:28.360
Yeah, so he was replaced by Riccardo Escotto.

25:29.240 --> 25:35.320
I mean, I think it’s for Barcelona cause they're both managed by the same person,

25:35.320 --> 25:41.000
so given how last minute this was, they had to really find somebody within literally a few days notice.

25:42.680 --> 25:45.400
So there is going to be somebody different for the Red Bull Ring.

25:45.400 --> 25:47.720
We believe there's going to be someone

25:47.720 --> 25:48.200
different.

25:48.200 --> 25:51.160
We believe. I mean, I'd love it for it to

25:51.160 --> 25:53.000
be KNB cause that would be hilarious.

25:53.000 --> 25:55.000
It would be so funny.

25:55.000 --> 25:58.520
Yeah, Riccardo Escotto, there's nothing

25:58.520 --> 26:03.640
very notable about him. I mean, he's currently losing to Niels Koolen in IndyNXT.

26:03.640 --> 26:07.640
How do you- How do you manage that?

26:08.120 --> 26:13.880
I mean, for reference, Koolen is in the better team, but still.

26:13.880 --> 26:18.520
His best finish in IndyNXT is P7,

26:21.560 --> 26:30.760
but yeah, not a very notable IndyNXT driver. I can't lie, when it was announced that he would

26:30.760 --> 26:35.800
be doing Barcelona, I was like, who the hell is that? I did not realize he was in IndyNXT.

26:35.800 --> 26:37.560
He's kind of a bit irrelevant.

26:37.560 --> 26:40.360
Yeah, but I think with AIX, for the next round,

26:40.360 --> 26:42.440
it’ll be the case of whoever can pay would get it.

26:42.440 --> 26:46.280
Who's the biggest pay driver in

26:46.280 --> 26:48.120
Formula Regional level at the moment?

26:48.120 --> 26:50.920
I'm going to say, look at F4, we have

26:50.920 --> 26:51.640
Maccagnani.

26:51.640 --> 26:54.760
Maccagnani is not going up to F3 for a round-

26:54.760 --> 26:57.480
I mean, Al Dhaheri? Al Dhaheri maybe?

26:57.480 --> 26:59.800
Potentially.

27:00.920 --> 27:05.240
We'll have to see. I swear to God if they announce it after the episode again, I'm going to-

27:05.240 --> 27:06.360
I don't know what I'm going to do.

27:06.360 --> 27:08.680
I mean, at least the entry list comes out

27:08.680 --> 27:10.440
next week rather than today.

27:10.440 --> 27:11.880
Yeah, exactly.

27:11.880 --> 27:14.280
Yeah, so looking at practice,

27:14.280 --> 27:21.160
nothing too notable, but oh my God, the Campos’. 1-2-3 What is this Campos rocket trip?

27:21.160 --> 27:24.360
I would much rather Campos domination

27:24.360 --> 27:29.160
than Trident domination, just because it means that if Alex Powell joins them next year,

27:30.040 --> 27:36.600
he's going to be good. Just trust me. I really want Alex Powell to join them next year.

27:36.600 --> 27:41.560
Um, quali I remember the ARTs and the VARs were

27:41.560 --> 27:42.360
really good.

27:42.360 --> 27:45.160
Yeah, and the Campos’. I mean,

27:45.160 --> 27:56.120
Campos were 1- they were 1-2-7. And the VARs, all of the VARs were 3-6-8. I think it's quite

27:56.120 --> 28:00.040
clear to me who the top two teams are. It's Campos and VAR, and that is-

28:00.040 --> 28:00.520
Really?

28:00.520 --> 28:02.920
I mean, at least this weekend.

28:02.920 --> 28:06.520
Yeah, I think- I don't know if VAR's

28:06.520 --> 28:08.520
performance this weekend was a fluke or not.

28:08.520 --> 28:11.800
VAR are second in the team's championship.

28:11.800 --> 28:14.120
Yeah, but they've gotten a lot of their

28:14.120 --> 28:23.320
points from the sprints. I mean, Del Pino has two sprint podiums. Yamakoshi ended up on a sprint

28:23.320 --> 28:26.280
podium. Deligny ended up on a sprint podium.

28:26.280 --> 28:29.960
I mean, I am biased as a Freddie fan because

28:29.960 --> 28:35.400
he was only P10 in quali, but I do think that Trident have taken a step back from where they

28:35.400 --> 28:41.000
were last year because I look at where Stromsted is. Stromsted is in the same team and is doing

28:41.000 --> 28:42.760
worse this year than he was last year.

28:42.760 --> 28:44.920
Or maybe your goat is washed.

28:44.920 --> 28:48.680
Maybe, but I'm just looking at Stromsted

28:48.760 --> 28:54.520
as my reference point. And it's clear, yes, Campos have good drivers, but also, I mean,

28:55.960 --> 29:02.040
the Campos’ just seem fully locked in in their cars and how it is. And yeah, Freddie was, but all

29:02.040 --> 29:06.200
the Tridents were kind of washed this weekend. I don't want to talk about the race with Freddie.

29:07.000 --> 29:11.880
I mean, I wanna talk about the sprint race, you know, he was P2 in the sprint race.

29:11.880 --> 29:15.240
I think the sprint was kind of really weird

29:15.240 --> 29:18.040
because we had James Wharton winning.

29:20.120 --> 29:29.000
Prema is good bro calm. We had surprisingly clean starts, at least for the sprint race.

29:29.000 --> 29:32.600
I don't know about the feature race. I was not there for the start of the feature race.

29:32.600 --> 29:34.600
I think they’re still traumatized from the

29:34.600 --> 29:35.800
Monaco sprint race.

29:35.800 --> 29:47.480
But yeah, so surprisingly clean start. I laughed when Ugo spun behind Enzo. He was

29:47.480 --> 29:49.320
really rattled by Enzo's aura.

29:49.320 --> 29:54.360
I mean, again, it was Turn 3, Turn 3 and Campos just weren't mixing this week.

29:54.360 --> 29:57.080
I know it wasn't because of Enzo's aura,

29:57.080 --> 29:59.320
but in my head it was because of Enzo's aura.

29:59.320 --> 30:03.080
Oh, yeah, no, he was just, he saw Deligny

30:03.080 --> 30:06.920
and was like, oh my god, he's gonna 6-7 me.

30:06.920 --> 30:17.480
Maybe Enzo did a 6-7 before the corner and then he felt it.

30:17.480 --> 30:20.040
I mean, knowing Enzo, he probably did.

30:20.040 --> 30:26.440
Both Rivera and Colnaghi ended up DNFing, which is really sad.

30:26.440 --> 30:32.280
For Rivera it was actually quite interesting, ‘cause Rivera had an electrical issue

30:32.280 --> 30:37.480
and that caused Badoer to go into him and initially Badoer was given a penalty for

30:37.480 --> 30:42.360
that, but then Campos showed really good sportsmanship by basically telling the

30:42.360 --> 30:48.040
stewards, they triggered a right of review saying, it's not his fault, it's the other car's fault,

30:48.040 --> 30:49.560
which you know, I like that.

30:49.560 --> 30:54.760
Colnaghi, I don't know what happened to him, but he like went into the pit lane

30:54.760 --> 30:59.480
in the beginning of the race and then I don't think he stopped in the pit lane, I think he

30:59.960 --> 31:04.200
went on to the end of the pit lane so he started in the pit lane for whatever reason

31:04.920 --> 31:08.200
and then he went back into the pits.

31:08.200 --> 31:11.880
He stalled on the formation.

31:11.880 --> 31:13.000
Did he?

31:13.000 --> 31:15.240
Yeah. Someone stalled.

31:15.240 --> 31:16.680
I don't remember.

31:16.680 --> 31:21.080
And then the feature race, which I don't really want to talk about because-

31:21.080 --> 31:23.000
Because your goat is washed.

31:23.800 --> 31:33.080
The feature race podium was Nael, Yamakoshi and Ugochukwu. Badoer was up there, I think he's

31:33.080 --> 31:36.360
been pretty consistently good so far this season.

31:36.360 --> 31:40.920
Yeah, he has. He's kind of been silently in the back. He's just kind of lurking there.

31:40.920 --> 31:46.120
Yeah, I was not expecting that going into the season, but I've been pleasantly surprised.

31:46.120 --> 31:51.240
I mean, maybe with some drivers, you know, they just take one season to get

31:51.240 --> 31:54.760
settled and then the second season, you know, they fully know and I think also maybe changing

31:54.760 --> 31:55.720
teams has helped him.

31:55.720 --> 31:58.280
Yeah, I mean, he was with Prema last year, wasn't he?

31:58.280 --> 32:03.480
But also, like you know, Ugochukwu and Leon are both. I mean, Ugo is a very good driver,

32:03.480 --> 32:08.040
Leon is solid, but in Rodin and maybe it's more of a relaxed environment as well,

32:08.040 --> 32:12.920
there's not as much pressure. He's no longer with McLaren as well, that pressure from an F1 team

32:12.920 --> 32:13.320
is gone.

32:13.320 --> 32:19.800
But yeah, so the VARs again were really good. I mean, we had Yamakoshi P2,

32:20.440 --> 32:25.960
Deligny P5 and Del Pino P6. It would have been really funny if they were 6th and 7th,

32:25.960 --> 32:27.800
but they're not. So whatever.

32:27.800 --> 32:30.280
They are 6th and 7th in the standings though.

32:30.280 --> 32:33.640
Yeah, Deligny and Yamakoshi are 6th and 7th.

32:33.640 --> 32:39.320
But like you see the top seven, I mean, all three Campos’, all three VARs.

32:39.320 --> 32:40.920
And then Badoer.

32:40.920 --> 32:45.080
Yeah. And Freddie, would’ve been up there if he just wasn’t washed.

32:45.080 --> 32:52.120
He'd already been overtaken by Deligny before his penalty.

32:52.120 --> 32:56.280
He would have been 7th without the penalty.

32:56.280 --> 33:01.560
It's not that bad of a drop. I mean, he's, where is he? 9th?

33:01.560 --> 33:05.880
He was 8th. He gained one spot. But if he gained one spot,

33:05.880 --> 33:08.680
that would mean he was P3 and points, not P4.

33:08.680 --> 33:12.200
He'll make it up. It's fine, whatever.

33:12.920 --> 33:18.280
I mean, I can't lie, I don't remember much from the feature race. But the championship is looking

33:18.280 --> 33:25.080
quite interesting, I think, because the top four are only separated by 11 points.

33:25.080 --> 33:32.920
I still wonder what Campos are going to do if Ugo and Theo end up in a title fight.

33:32.920 --> 33:40.120
Yeah, I mean, even 17 points is the gap if we include Badoer, which I mean, I can't lie,

33:40.120 --> 33:42.280
I would have thought Ugo would have had a bigger lead.

33:42.280 --> 33:46.920
I think that spin in the sprint didn't really help him.

33:46.920 --> 33:50.520
And also losing out to P2, to Yamakoshi,

33:50.520 --> 33:55.400
not being on the podium in Monaco. But I mean, it's left this championship fairly wide open.

33:56.200 --> 34:01.000
And like, Del Pino is still keeping up there. I think he's finished the sprint race and the

34:01.000 --> 34:04.120
feature race in the same spot, so I can no longer call him a sprint merchant.

34:04.120 --> 34:07.080
I am surprised Del Pino is up there.

34:08.040 --> 34:13.000
I think also, I mean, maybe once we get to more of the traditional races,

34:13.000 --> 34:17.320
because you know, Melbourne and Monaco, both street circuits, is not entirely

34:18.200 --> 34:19.480
representative. But

34:19.480 --> 34:24.520
Both Deligny and Del Pino finished the sprint and the future in the same spots,

34:25.320 --> 34:26.760
fifth and sixth for both.

34:26.760 --> 34:29.560
It should have been one spot lower each of you.

34:29.560 --> 34:33.400
More points for Enzo, I'm happy with that.

34:33.960 --> 34:39.400
Yeah, Enzo is on 28, I think. Yeah, they're quite a step back from the top five,

34:39.400 --> 34:41.480
but you never know what could happen.

34:41.480 --> 34:49.160
I saw the championship standings after, what is this, round three, four?

34:49.160 --> 34:50.760
Three out of nine.

34:50.760 --> 34:57.000
I saw the championship standings after Imola last season. And you know who was in

34:57.560 --> 35:02.760
P6 in the standings at that time? It was Nikola Tsolov. Where did Nikola Tsolov finish that

35:02.760 --> 35:04.600
championship? P2?

35:04.600 --> 35:10.280
Exactly. Enzo is going to end up P2 in the championship. You heard it here first, trust me.

35:10.280 --> 35:14.120
